# Cursor Rules for Teams Notification API Project

## Core Behavior Rules

`DO NOT GIVE ME HIGH LEVEL SHIT, IF I ASK FOR FIX OR EXPLANATION, I WANT ACTUAL CODE OR EXPLANATION!!! I DON'T WANT "Here's how you can blablabla"`

- Be casual unless otherwise specified
- Be terse
- Suggest solutions that I didn't think about—anticipate my needs
- Treat me as an expert
- Be accurate and thorough
- Give the answer immediately. Provide detailed explanations and restate my query in your own words if necessary after giving the answer
- Value good arguments over authorities, the source is irrelevant
- Consider new technologies and contrarian ideas, not just the conventional wisdom
- You may use high levels of speculation or prediction, just flag it for me
- No moral lectures
- Discuss safety only when it's crucial and non-obvious
- If your content policy is an issue, provide the closest acceptable response and explain the content policy issue afterward
- Cite sources whenever possible at the end, not inline
- No need to mention your knowledge cutoff
- No need to disclose you're an AI
- Please respect my prettier preferences when you provide code.
- Split into multiple responses if one response isn't enough to answer the question.

If I ask for adjustments to code I have provided you, do not repeat all of my code unnecessarily. Instead try to keep the answer brief by giving just a couple lines before/after any changes you make. Multiple code blocks are ok.

---

## Bash Commands

### Development
- `make docker-up` - Start PostgreSQL and Redis containers
- `make docker-down` - Stop Docker containers
- `make db-init` - Initialize database schema and seed data
- `make build` - Build the Go application
- `make run` - Run the application (loads .env automatically)
- `make test` - Run all Go tests
- `make clean` - Clean build artifacts

### Testing
- `make test-e2e` - Run E2E tests
- `make test-load` - Run load tests with k6
- `make test-api` - Run API integration tests
- `make test-report` - Generate test coverage reports
- `make test-full` - Run complete test suite

### Code Quality
- `go fmt ./...` - Format all Go code
- `go vet ./...` - Run go vet static analysis
- `golangci-lint run` - Run linter (if installed)
- `go test -cover ./...` - Run tests with coverage report

### Documentation
- `./scripts/utils/check-doc-sync.sh` - Check documentation sync with code
- Always update docs after feature changes

---

## Code Style

### Go Code
- Use `golangci-lint` for code quality checks
- Follow Go naming conventions (short, concise, semantic)
- Use `go fmt` before committing
- Prefer explicit error handling over panics
- Use context.Context for cancellation and timeouts
- Module structure:
  ```
  /cmd/server/          # Service entry point
  /services/            # Business logic
  /configs/             # Configuration files
  /scripts/             # Scripts and utilities
  ```

### Frontend (React/TypeScript)
- Use TypeScript strict mode
- Prefer functional components with hooks
- Use ES modules (import/export) syntax
- Destructure imports when possible: `import { foo } from 'bar'`
- Follow React best practices (keys, memoization, etc.)

### General
- Use meaningful variable and function names
- Keep functions small and focused
- Add comments for complex logic, not obvious code
- Prefer composition over inheritance

---

## Incremental Development Workflow

### Feature Development Checklist

When developing a new feature or refactoring, ALWAYS follow this workflow:

#### 1. Planning Phase
- [ ] Understand requirements and scope
- [ ] Check existing similar implementations
- [ ] Plan API changes (if any)
- [ ] Identify affected files and dependencies

#### 2. Development Phase
- [ ] Write code following project style guide
- [ ] Add inline comments for complex logic
- [ ] Ensure error handling is comprehensive
- [ ] Keep commits atomic and meaningful

#### 3. Testing Phase (REQUIRED)
- [ ] Write unit tests for new functions/methods
- [ ] Write integration tests for API endpoints
- [ ] Run `make test` and ensure all tests pass
- [ ] Check test coverage: `go test -cover ./...`
- [ ] Test edge cases and error scenarios
- [ ] Run `make test-e2e` for end-to-end validation
- [ ] If performance-critical, run `make test-load`

#### 4. Documentation Phase (REQUIRED)
- [ ] Update API documentation if endpoints changed
- [ ] Update `docs/06_USER_GUIDE/` if user-facing features changed
- [ ] Update `docs/02_ARCHITECTURE/` if architecture changed
- [ ] Update `docs/03_DESIGN/` if design patterns changed
- [ ] Run `./scripts/utils/check-doc-sync.sh` to verify sync
- [ ] Add code examples in documentation
- [ ] Update CHANGELOG if significant changes

#### 5. Code Quality Phase
- [ ] Run `go fmt ./...` to format code
- [ ] Run `go vet ./...` to check for issues
- [ ] Run `golangci-lint run` if available
- [ ] Review code for potential improvements
- [ ] Ensure no debug logs or commented code remain

#### 6. Commit Phase
- [ ] Use conventional commit format: `<type>(<scope>): <description>`
- [ ] Types: `feat`, `fix`, `docs`, `refactor`, `test`, `chore`
- [ ] Include test results in commit message if significant
- [ ] Reference related issues/PRs if applicable

### Refactoring Checklist

When refactoring existing code:
- [ ] Ensure all existing tests pass before refactoring
- [ ] Add new tests for refactored code paths
- [ ] Maintain backward compatibility when possible
- [ ] Update affected documentation
- [ ] Run full test suite: `make test-full`
- [ ] Verify no performance regression

### Before Committing
- [ ] All tests pass: `make test`
- [ ] Code is formatted: `go fmt ./...`
- [ ] No linter errors
- [ ] Documentation is updated
- [ ] Commit message follows convention

---

## Testing Requirements

### Test Coverage Standards
- Unit test coverage: ≥ 80% for new code
- Integration tests: Required for all API endpoints
- E2E tests: Required for critical user flows
- Load tests: Required for performance-critical features

### Test Best Practices
- Write tests before or alongside code (TDD preferred)
- Test both success and failure cases
- Use descriptive test names: `TestFunctionName_Scenario_ExpectedResult`
- Keep tests independent and isolated
- Use table-driven tests for multiple scenarios
- Mock external dependencies (database, APIs, etc.)

### Test Execution
- Run tests frequently during development
- Run full test suite before committing
- Use `go test -v` for verbose output during debugging
- Use `go test -race` to detect race conditions

---

## Documentation Standards

### When to Update Documentation
- **Always** when adding/modifying API endpoints
- **Always** when changing user-facing features
- **Always** when modifying architecture or design patterns
- **Always** when adding new configuration options
- **Always** when changing deployment procedures

### Documentation Structure
- API docs: `docs/03_DESIGN/` (API specifications)
- Architecture: `docs/02_ARCHITECTURE/`
- User guides: `docs/06_USER_GUIDE/`
- Development: `docs/07_DEVELOPMENT/`
- Testing: `docs/04_TEST/`
- Deployment: `docs/05_DEPLOYMENT/`

### Documentation Quality
- Include working code examples
- Keep examples up-to-date with actual code
- Use clear, concise language
- Add diagrams when helpful
- Include troubleshooting sections

---

## Project-Specific Rules

### Teams Notification API
- Backend: Go 1.24+, PostgreSQL 15+, Redis 7+
- Frontend: React 19+, TypeScript, Vite
- Use Actor pattern for async notification processing
- Follow microservices architecture principles
- Maintain backward compatibility for API v1

### Database
- Use migrations for schema changes
- Never modify production schema directly
- Test migrations on staging first
- Document schema changes

### API Design
- RESTful conventions for endpoints
- Use proper HTTP status codes
- Include request/response examples
- Version APIs: `/api/v1/...`
- Document rate limits and quotas

### Error Handling
- Return structured error responses
- Include error codes and messages
- Log errors with context
- Don't expose internal errors to clients

---

## VSCode Best Practices

### Extensions Recommended
- Go extension (golang.go)
- ESLint for TypeScript/React
- Prettier for code formatting
- GitLens for Git integration
- Error Lens for inline errors

### Editor Settings
- Format on save enabled
- Auto-save enabled
- Use workspace settings when possible
- Configure linters to match project standards

### Development Workflow
- Use VSCode tasks for common commands
- Use debugger for Go applications
- Use integrated terminal for running tests
- Use source control panel for Git operations

